{{Nihongo|Democratic Socialist Party of Okaiken|民主社会党|Okaiken Minshu Shakai-tō}} is a social-democratic political party in [[Okaiken]]. Historically one of the three pillars of the Sanese political system, the party has faced a severe decline in influence since the turn of the century. It is most notable in contemporary politics as the former party of [[Aono Michifumi]], the current [[Governor of Okami]], who was a member from 2011 until his resignation in 2018.

==History==
===Foundation and early years===
The party was established in 1964 following a split from the [[Socialist Party of Okaiken]]. The founding members, led by industrial labor organizers, rejected the  platform of [[Genzui Kusaka]] in favor of a democratic socialist model that supported a mixed economy and the Sanese–XXX Security Treaty. Throughout the late 20th century, the party maintained a consistent presence in the [[National Diet of Okaiken]], representing the interests of organized labor in the [[Tarumi]] and [[Tatara]] industrial corridors.

===The 2013 general election===
The party entered a period of terminal decline following the 2013 general election. Internal disagreements over consumption tax increases and a failure to modernize its platform led to a massive loss of support. Before the election, the party held 8 seats in the Parliament; however, the results saw this number plummet to just 2 seats. This defeat stripped the party of its status as a political force, leading to a decade of marginalization and financial instability.

===Departure of Aono Michifumi===
Aono Michifumi joined the party in 2011 as a legal advisor for the party's housing task force. He was elected to the [[Okami Metropolitan Assembly]] representing the Tatara 2nd district under the party banner in 2016. Aono quickly became a polarizing figure within the party leadership due to his advocacy for radical rent controls and his critique of the party's reliance on aging industrial unions. 

In 2018, Aono officially left the party to run as an independent, citing the leadership's refusal to endorse his "People's Metropolis Plan." His departure triggered a mass exodus of the party's youth wing and student organizers, many of whom formed the backbone of his 2024 gubernatorial campaign.

==Ideology and policies==
The party officially advocates for social democracy, though it is often categorized as more conservative on security and foreign policy than the [[Socialist Party of Okaiken]]. Its core pillars include:
* Protection of the collective bargaining rights of the [[Okaiken Federation of Labor]].
* Maintenance of the national universal healthcare system.
* Support for small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) through tax incentives.
* Gradual transition to renewable energy while protecting jobs in the traditional energy sector.

